{"type":"WebSearch","query":"PPIC poll California Proposition 38 October 2026 immunology bonds yes no","content":"**In the October 2026 PPIC Statewide Survey (Californians and Their Government), 57% of likely voters said they would vote yes on California Proposition 38 (authorizing $8.4 billion in general obligation bonds for immunology and immunotherapy medical research), while 41% said no (with ~2% undecided).**[[1]](https://www.ppic.org/publication/ppic-statewide-survey-californians-and-their-government-october-2026/)

This is based on interviews conducted October 16–22, 2026, among 1,086 likely voters (margin of error ±3.8% at 95% confidence). Support was similar to the September 2026 PPIC poll (55% yes). Partisans were divided: 70% of Democrats, 53% of independents, and 37% of Republicans supported it. Majorities backed it across regions and most demographic groups.[[1]](https://www.ppic.org/publication/ppic-statewide-survey-californians-and-their-government-october-2026/)

Proposition 38 is a citizens’ initiative to fund research (roughly half to a UC-affiliated institute and half via competitive grants) aimed at diseases like cancer, Alzheimer’s, and others. The survey asked after respondents read the ballot title and label. Full crosstabs, methodology, and the report are available on the PPIC website.","citations":["https://www.ppic.org/publication/ppic-statewide-survey-californians-and-their-government-october-2026/"],"allowed_domains":null}
